43
00:01:52,520 --> 00:01:55,040
or I'll organize the
material and reasoning

44
00:01:55,040 --> 00:01:57,520
and, especially RL for
reasoning, in a particular way

45
00:01:57,520 --> 00:01:59,030
that I particularly like.

46
00:01:59,030 --> 00:02:02,005
And to do that, we'll start
off from the conventional way

47
00:02:02,005 --> 00:02:03,630
or looking back at
the conventional way

48
00:02:03,630 --> 00:02:05,185
of training language models.

49
00:02:05,185 --> 00:02:07,310
So typically, when we train
language models or just

50
00:02:07,310 --> 00:02:09,310
generally foundation
models overall,

51
00:02:09,310 --> 00:02:11,350
the conventional
paradigm has been

52
00:02:11,350 --> 00:02:14,310
to do next token prediction,
meaning you take your data.

53
00:02:14,310 --> 00:02:19,130
You tokenize that into a string
of tokens and token inputs,

54
00:02:19,130 --> 00:02:22,350
and you train the model with
a negative log likelihood loss

55
00:02:22,350 --> 00:02:24,770
to predict the next token
that appears in the sequence.

56
00:02:24,770 --> 00:02:26,270
So in the context
of reasoning, this

57
00:02:26,270 --> 00:02:28,370
would mean you would
take the question.

58
00:02:28,370 --> 00:02:30,570
You would tokenize it
into a string of tokens.

59
00:02:30,570 --> 00:02:33,070
You'll take the answer, which
is from one of these reference

60
00:02:33,070 --> 00:02:35,170
solutions, tokenize that
into a string of tokens,

61
00:02:35,170 --> 00:02:36,628
and then train the
model to predict

62
00:02:36,628 --> 00:02:40,830
this answer string conditioned
on the input question string.

63
00:02:40,830 --> 00:02:43,350
Now, to start to
look at, or start

64
00:02:43,350 --> 00:02:45,550
to understand why
RL matters here,

65
00:02:45,550 --> 00:02:47,230
we'll start by
asking this question

66
00:02:47,230 --> 00:02:49,790
of why does next token
prediction of this form

67
00:02:49,790 --> 00:02:51,190
actually work?

68
00:02:51,190 --> 00:02:53,950
And again, this
answering this question

69
00:02:53,950 --> 00:02:56,750
will take more than--
it's an open research

70
00:02:56,750 --> 00:02:58,070
question to be honest.

71
00:02:58,070 --> 00:03:01,190
But one perspective of looking
at this that I particularly like

72
00:03:01,190 --> 00:03:03,910
is to look at this
particular equation, which

73
00:03:03,910 --> 00:03:07,430
tells you how well next token
prediction works in general.

74
00:03:07,430 --> 00:03:09,270
So what is this
equation telling me?

75
00:03:09,270 --> 00:03:11,510
It's telling me that if I
train a model, if I train

76
00:03:11,510 --> 00:03:14,930
a particular model p theta,
which is mapping some inputs x,

77
00:03:14,930 --> 00:03:18,910
like the question, to some
output y, like the answer,

78
00:03:18,910 --> 00:03:22,430
this learned model is only
going to approximate the ground

79
00:03:22,430 --> 00:03:25,830
truth underlying model, or
the ground truth solution,

80
00:03:25,830 --> 00:03:29,350
with an error, meaning
with an error that

81
00:03:29,350 --> 00:03:31,750
is shown on the slide, which
is p theta minus p star,

82
00:03:31,750 --> 00:03:35,110
depending on the size of the
data that you use for training.

83
00:03:35,110 --> 00:03:36,950
So if you have a
lot of data which

84
00:03:36,950 --> 00:03:40,208
shows you many, many questions
and many, many answers,

85
00:03:40,208 --> 00:03:41,750
then ideally this
error would be low,

86
00:03:41,750 --> 00:03:45,230
and you would approximate a
very highly capable expert

87
00:03:45,230 --> 00:03:46,950
at solving these questions.

88
00:03:46,950 --> 00:03:49,680
But in reality, it so happens
that the amount of data

89
00:03:49,680 --> 00:03:51,430
that you have for a
lot of these reasoning

90
00:03:51,430 --> 00:03:54,470
problems, many other
domains as well,

91
00:03:54,470 --> 00:03:57,600
you really don't have enough
data to make this bound

92
00:03:57,600 --> 00:03:59,620
small enough, so
you end up learning

93
00:03:59,620 --> 00:04:01,100
models that are
not approximating

94
00:04:01,100 --> 00:04:04,740
human-level capabilities if you
just do next token prediction.

95
00:04:04,740 --> 00:04:07,100
So there's actually
a lot of work

96
00:04:07,100 --> 00:04:10,013
that exists out there which
studies the amount of data.

97
00:04:10,013 --> 00:04:12,180
And one of the interesting
things that they tell you

98
00:04:12,180 --> 00:04:14,580
is if you simply keep doing--

99
00:04:14,580 --> 00:04:17,100
if you simply keep taking
high-quality solutions

100
00:04:17,100 --> 00:04:20,839
of hard questions
from the internet,

101
00:04:20,839 --> 00:04:24,200
and you train your model with
some supervised training,

102
00:04:24,200 --> 00:04:27,100
some next token
prediction, you can not

103
00:04:27,100 --> 00:04:28,757
do really well
because it's estimated

104
00:04:28,757 --> 00:04:31,340
that you're going to run out of
high-quality data of this sort

105
00:04:31,340 --> 00:04:32,520
by 2028.

106
00:04:32,520 --> 00:04:34,460
And clearly, have not
gotten to a level where

107
00:04:34,460 --> 00:04:37,080
you can do great research
with-- or these models,

108
00:04:37,080 --> 00:04:40,100
can just replace human
researchers, for instance.

109
00:04:40,100 --> 00:04:41,660
This also happens
in other domains,

110
00:04:41,660 --> 00:04:44,200
like for instance, in robotics
and embodied AI where, again,

111
00:04:44,200 --> 00:04:45,740
data is very
limited, which means

112
00:04:45,740 --> 00:04:48,500
that if you just simply rely
on next token prediction

113
00:04:48,500 --> 00:04:51,900
alone, you'll not be able to get
the ground truth expert level

114
00:04:51,900 --> 00:04:55,620
capability in your
model of sorts.

115
00:04:55,620 --> 00:04:58,500
So just to give you
a concrete example,

116
00:04:58,500 --> 00:05:00,367
if you just train
models on current data,

117
00:05:00,367 --> 00:05:02,700
and you ask them to solve
this very interesting question

118
00:05:02,700 --> 00:05:05,555
from IMO 2021,
presumably this question

119
00:05:05,555 --> 00:05:07,680
exists in the training sets
of one of these models.

120
00:05:07,680 --> 00:05:12,000
But if you ask GPT or OpenAI
family of models to solve this,

121
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:14,260
they're going to give you
a solution that looks very

122
00:05:14,260 --> 00:05:16,038
sensible in these settings.

123
00:05:16,038 --> 00:05:17,580
So they're going to
produce solutions

124
00:05:17,580 --> 00:05:20,700
that look quite like a solution
that a human expert would have

125
00:05:20,700 --> 00:05:23,180
written, except that
they end up missing

126
00:05:23,180 --> 00:05:24,925
the logic behind the answer.

127
00:05:24,925 --> 00:05:27,300
So in this particular question,
if you need to prove that

128
00:05:27,300 --> 00:05:29,160
inequality that you
see up on the slide,

129
00:05:29,160 --> 00:05:32,020
you cannot really go out there
and start to look at individual

130
00:05:32,020 --> 00:05:33,560
terms of that expression.

131
00:05:33,560 --> 00:05:35,380
You have to think about
the whole summation

132
00:05:35,380 --> 00:05:36,880
as a whole and these models.

133
00:05:36,880 --> 00:05:39,240
This is, I think, from
an O series model--

134
00:05:39,240 --> 00:05:43,180
I think O1 that I
got the solution.

135
00:05:43,180 --> 00:05:46,020
It starts to look at
individual terms, for instance,

136
00:05:46,020 --> 00:05:49,898
and ends up asserting
its solution at the end.

137
00:05:49,898 --> 00:05:51,940
But it really does not
realize that it's actually

138
00:05:51,940 --> 00:05:54,315
making a mistake and
does not correct it.

139
00:05:54,315 --> 00:05:55,690
So really, what
ends up happening

140
00:05:55,690 --> 00:05:57,890
with these current supervised
training paradigms, when

141
00:05:57,890 --> 00:05:59,370
you think about
reasoning, is that they're

142
00:05:59,370 --> 00:06:01,130
going to produce a
solution that looks very

143
00:06:01,130 --> 00:06:02,810
much like a legitimate
solution that you

144
00:06:02,810 --> 00:06:04,330
could have written on an exam.

145
00:06:04,330 --> 00:06:06,270
It's just that solution
ends up being wrong,

146
00:06:06,270 --> 00:06:10,370
and the logical content of that
solution is not quite correct.

147
00:06:10,370 --> 00:06:13,550
And that's precisely something
that RL tries to fix.

148
00:06:13,550 --> 00:06:14,970
And I'll hopefully
try to show you

149
00:06:14,970 --> 00:06:17,450
some examples today
of what kind of RL

150
00:06:17,450 --> 00:06:20,850
techniques come to
rescue in that setting.

151
00:06:20,850 --> 00:06:23,290
So in today's
lecture, I guess we'll

152
00:06:23,290 --> 00:06:24,770
talk about RL for reasoning.

153
00:06:24,770 --> 00:06:29,030
I roughly characterized
this in two parts,

154
00:06:29,030 --> 00:06:30,890
or I broke this
down into two parts.

155
00:06:30,890 --> 00:06:33,110
And these two parts
are based on timeline.

156
00:06:33,110 --> 00:06:37,610
So it's based on stuff that
