199
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:13,560
thinking models that have been
doing really amazing right now.

200
00:08:13,560 --> 00:08:16,280
And there's some very
interesting things

201
00:08:16,280 --> 00:08:19,040
that need to change when you go
from the first on the left hand

202
00:08:19,040 --> 00:08:23,440
side to the right hand side
in this particular setting.

203
00:08:23,440 --> 00:08:27,920
So I guess I already talked
about the problem setup,

204
00:08:27,920 --> 00:08:31,520
but let me just quickly go
into a little bit of a detail

205
00:08:31,520 --> 00:08:34,159
and connect back
to RL terminology.

206
00:08:34,159 --> 00:08:36,850
So we're going to
consider reasoning.

207
00:08:36,850 --> 00:08:39,100
And in this particular setting
as I described earlier,

208
00:08:39,100 --> 00:08:41,100
you will be given a problem
in natural language,

209
00:08:41,100 --> 00:08:43,679
and you have to write a
solution in natural language.

210
00:08:43,679 --> 00:08:46,400
So what we can do when you think
about this in the context of RL

211
00:08:46,400 --> 00:08:48,360
from an RL person's
lens, is you can

212
00:08:48,360 --> 00:08:51,220
think of this whole
problem solving as an MDP,

213
00:08:51,220 --> 00:08:53,280
as a Markov Decision Process.

214
00:08:53,280 --> 00:08:54,800
And to do that,
what you can do is

215
00:08:54,800 --> 00:08:56,400
you can think of
the string of tokens

216
00:08:56,400 --> 00:08:58,240
that exist in the--
or string of words

217
00:08:58,240 --> 00:09:00,640
that exist in the
question as sort

218
00:09:00,640 --> 00:09:03,160
of an initial state of your MDP.

219
00:09:03,160 --> 00:09:05,520
So it's the initial state
that your agent or your policy

220
00:09:05,520 --> 00:09:10,000
is going to start from when
it starts to act in the world.

221
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:13,640
You can think of the solution
that the model produces

222
00:09:13,640 --> 00:09:16,920
as being broken down
into a sequence of steps,

223
00:09:16,920 --> 00:09:19,665
and these steps could correspond
to actions, for example.

224
00:09:19,665 --> 00:09:21,040
So you can think
of it as there's

225
00:09:21,040 --> 00:09:23,615
a model, which takes as input
this initial state, which

226
00:09:23,615 --> 00:09:24,240
is the problem.

227
00:09:24,240 --> 00:09:26,720
It, one by one, takes
in a bunch of actions

228
00:09:26,720 --> 00:09:28,680
which are these
steps, and at the end

229
00:09:28,680 --> 00:09:30,620
ends up with a
particular answer.

230
00:09:30,620 --> 00:09:32,480
So in this particular
setting, that answer

231
00:09:32,480 --> 00:09:37,320
is represented in this
box that you see up there.

232
00:09:37,320 --> 00:09:38,458
And the answer is 8.

233
00:09:38,458 --> 00:09:40,000
And what you're
going to do is you're

234
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:44,640
going to reward the model
with a sparse reward of plus 1

235
00:09:44,640 --> 00:09:48,870
if this answer ends up being
correct, and a reward of 0

236
00:09:48,870 --> 00:09:51,070
if this answer does not
end up being correct.

237
00:09:51,070 --> 00:09:54,630
So the real Markov decision
process here is state.

238
00:09:54,630 --> 00:09:56,750
I'm going to a
sequence of actions

239
00:09:56,750 --> 00:10:01,350
and going to a termination
bit where the model produces

240
00:10:01,350 --> 00:10:04,030
an answer and you get a
reward at the end of it

241
00:10:04,030 --> 00:10:08,670
if the particular answer
is correct or not.

242
00:10:08,670 --> 00:10:12,150
So in RL terminology, this is
a lot like a sparse reward MDP

243
00:10:12,150 --> 00:10:14,790
with a deterministic dynamics.

244
00:10:14,790 --> 00:10:15,750
What do I mean by this?

245
00:10:15,750 --> 00:10:18,532
Basically, there's no
external environment

246
00:10:18,532 --> 00:10:20,490
in this whole process of
producing these steps.

247
00:10:20,490 --> 00:10:23,390
There's no external environment
that gives you a next state.

248
00:10:23,390 --> 00:10:26,390
All you are seeing is the
initial state and these actions

249
00:10:26,390 --> 00:10:29,330
one by ond, which tell you that
the next state of the problem.

250
00:10:29,330 --> 00:10:32,430
So I'll just draw on the
board because I think--

251
00:10:32,430 --> 00:10:35,110
so this is your initial state.

252
00:10:35,110 --> 00:10:38,330
Your model takes as action,
the particular step.

253
00:10:38,330 --> 00:10:39,830
The first step of
the problem, it

254
00:10:39,830 --> 00:10:41,470
gets to state S1,
which is nothing

255
00:10:41,470 --> 00:10:45,150
but the string of
tokens in S0 and S0

256
00:10:45,150 --> 00:10:46,650
concatenated with each other.

257
00:10:46,650 --> 00:10:48,190
So that's all S1 is.

258
00:10:48,190 --> 00:10:51,050
It produces the next
state A1 or next--

259
00:10:51,050 --> 00:10:54,970
it takes the next step A1 or
next action A1 goes to S2.

260
00:10:54,970 --> 00:10:58,750
And your next state
S2 is just S0, A0, A1

261
00:10:58,750 --> 00:11:01,970
concatenated with each other
until you hit the final answer.

262
00:11:01,970 --> 00:11:03,410
So you do this multiple times.

263
00:11:03,410 --> 00:11:04,670
You hit the final answer.

264
00:11:04,670 --> 00:11:07,070
Your model produces some
answer-- let's say 8.

265
00:11:07,070 --> 00:11:08,790
You get a reward
which is based on

266
00:11:08,790 --> 00:11:13,230
whether 8 is equal to the
ground truth answer or not.

267
00:11:13,230 --> 00:11:15,930
So you get a reward of r
of 8 comma ground truth.

268
00:11:15,930 --> 00:11:17,870
This is either plus 1 or 0.

269
00:11:17,870 --> 00:11:20,438
That's your MDP that
you're operating in.

270
00:11:20,438 --> 00:11:21,730
So that's a very good question.

271
00:11:21,730 --> 00:11:23,553
So the actions here
could be infinity.

272
00:11:23,553 --> 00:11:24,970
There are infinitely
many actions.

273
00:11:24,970 --> 00:11:28,150
But unlike having-- unlike
