456
00:19:38,510 --> 00:19:40,950
and Oracle solutions,
SFT, number of questions

457
00:19:40,950 --> 00:19:43,870
and correct solutions by
filtering or rejection, which

458
00:19:43,870 --> 00:19:45,710
is called Rejection
Fine Tuning, or RFP,

459
00:19:45,710 --> 00:19:47,630
and just training on
any kind of data, which

460
00:19:47,630 --> 00:19:49,165
is reinforcement learning.

461
00:19:49,165 --> 00:19:50,790
So what I'm going to
do in this lecture

462
00:19:50,790 --> 00:19:53,630
is we'll talk about how these
different methods actually

463
00:19:53,630 --> 00:19:55,510
behave in practice,
and that'll give us

464
00:19:55,510 --> 00:19:58,390
a sense of what to
use in what case.

465
00:19:58,390 --> 00:20:00,990
So the question is, are we
forcing the model to come up

466
00:20:00,990 --> 00:20:02,910
with overly verbose answers?

467
00:20:02,910 --> 00:20:04,830
So here we are not really--

468
00:20:04,830 --> 00:20:07,710
so the short answer is no
because here we are not really

469
00:20:07,710 --> 00:20:09,617
encouraging verbosity at all.

470
00:20:09,617 --> 00:20:11,950
All we're doing is we're
simply sampling multiple traces

471
00:20:11,950 --> 00:20:16,070
from a model and training
the model to find answers--

472
00:20:16,070 --> 00:20:19,830
train the model to closley
mimic traces, which ended up

473
00:20:19,830 --> 00:20:21,995
being successful in some way.

474
00:20:21,995 --> 00:20:23,870
So if the traces that
end up being successful

475
00:20:23,870 --> 00:20:26,750
are those that actually need
more space to be verbose,

476
00:20:26,750 --> 00:20:28,798
then it's going to
incentivize you to be verbose.

477
00:20:28,798 --> 00:20:30,590
But if the questions
that end up succeeding

478
00:20:30,590 --> 00:20:32,750
are not those-- or the
traces that end up succeeding

479
00:20:32,750 --> 00:20:34,760
are not the ones that end
up needing to be verbose,

