580
00:24:35,800 --> 00:24:37,880
for SFT.

581
00:24:37,880 --> 00:24:41,640
So what this tells you at a
high level is that on-policy

582
00:24:41,640 --> 00:24:44,480
imitation, learning, sampling
from your policy and imitating

583
00:24:44,480 --> 00:24:47,360
the correct answers can be
2x more efficient in this

584
00:24:47,360 --> 00:24:51,160
particular setting, compared
to SFT alone or off-policy SFT

585
00:24:51,160 --> 00:24:53,640
or just imitation
learning alone.

586
00:24:53,640 --> 00:24:54,500
OK, great.

587
00:24:54,500 --> 00:24:59,177
So that's the first result,
first takeaway to have here.

588
00:24:59,177 --> 00:25:00,760
But it so turns out
that if you simply

589
00:25:00,760 --> 00:25:02,720
keep doing this
over and over, it's

590
00:25:02,720 --> 00:25:05,960
not going to give you the best
possible policy or the best

591
00:25:05,960 --> 00:25:07,080
possible model.

592
00:25:07,080 --> 00:25:10,127
So if you simply keep scaling
up SFT on policy data,

593
00:25:10,127 --> 00:25:11,460
what do I mean by scaling it up?

