00:26:47,450 --> 00:26:49,710
But yeah, it's just
a constant multiplier

638
00:26:49,710 --> 00:26:52,845
which stays same for all the
points on that particular plot.

639
00:26:52,845 --> 00:26:53,970
So that's a great question.

640
00:26:53,970 --> 00:26:56,890
So the question is, when you
scale S tilde, which is for RFP,

641
00:26:56,890 --> 00:26:58,910
do you do deduplication?

642
00:26:58,910 --> 00:27:00,110
The answer is yes.

643
00:27:00,110 --> 00:27:02,110
And I actually removed
that detail from my slide

644
00:27:02,110 --> 00:27:03,770
because I thought it
would be too much details.

645
00:27:03,770 --> 00:27:05,390
But since you asked
it, the answer

646
00:27:05,390 --> 00:27:07,530
is yes, you would often--
when you do this process,

647
00:27:07,530 --> 00:27:10,112
you would often
deduplicate S tildes.

648
00:27:10,112 --> 00:27:11,570
There's a lot of
mechanisms for it.

649
00:27:11,570 --> 00:27:15,070
People can do just edit
distance-based rejection

650
00:27:15,070 --> 00:27:15,570
as well.

651
00:27:15,570 --> 00:27:17,850
So keep the sample more than n.

652
00:27:17,850 --> 00:27:20,990
So one of the recipes people do
is sample more than n, like, 2n,

653
00:27:20,990 --> 00:27:23,990
and retain the n of them, which
actually seem to be maximally

654
00:27:23,990 --> 00:27:24,620
diverse.

655
00:27:24,620 --> 00:27:26,370
That's something people
do a lot of times.

656
00:27:26,370 --> 00:27:28,670
So yeah, you do
that kind of thing.

657
00:27:28,670 --> 00:27:29,790
Great, OK.

658
00:27:29,790 --> 00:27:31,630
So what this tells
us at a high level

659
00:27:31,630 --> 00:27:33,590
is that fitting on
self-generated data

660
00:27:33,590 --> 00:27:36,310
on a limited number
of initial states

661
00:27:36,310 --> 00:27:38,610
actually hurts
generalization of the model.

662
00:27:38,610 --> 00:27:41,010
So if you recall the
picture that I drew earlier,

663
00:27:41,010 --> 00:27:44,510
which I erased, where there was
an S0, and your model took an A0

664
00:27:44,510 --> 00:27:46,990
and went to S1, you can think
of this as basically training

665
00:27:46,990 --> 00:27:49,910
on a set of S0 and
evaluating on a new set,

666
00:27:49,910 --> 00:27:52,490
on a new test problem.

667
00:27:52,490 --> 00:27:54,990
And turns out that if you train
too much on a particular set

668
00:27:54,990 --> 00:27:56,450
of S naughts in
this particular way,

669
00:27:56,450 --> 00:27:57,992
you end up hitting
the generalization

670
00:27:57,992 --> 00:28:01,230
bottleneck in that setting.

671
00:28:01,230 --> 00:28:02,610
OK, so why does this happen?

672
00:28:02,610 --> 00:28:04,978
And this is the picture that
I'm going to talk about.

673
00:28:04,978 --> 00:28:07,270
And I'll connect it to some
of the interesting concepts

674
00:28:07,270 --> 00:28:09,510
that we know in imitation
learning right now,

675
00:28:09,510 --> 00:28:12,110
which is this picture
of spurious steps.

676
00:28:12,110 --> 00:28:14,390
So yeah, so what
we're going to do

677
00:28:14,390 --> 00:28:17,070
is we'll move away a
little bit from examples

678
00:28:17,070 --> 00:28:18,830
and look at pictures.

679
00:28:18,830 --> 00:28:20,788
And what we'll do is
we'll plot these pictures,

680
00:28:20,788 --> 00:28:22,247
as I was describing
to you earlier,

681
00:28:22,247 --> 00:28:23,810
as this little
trajectory in an MDP.

682
00:28:23,810 --> 00:28:25,393
So you have prompts
as initial states,

683
00:28:25,393 --> 00:28:27,340
and you're producing
these steps one by one.

684
00:28:27,340 --> 00:28:28,548
They take you to next states.

685
00:28:28,548 --> 00:28:30,860
And then eventually, you
end up at the answer.

686
00:28:30,860 --> 00:28:33,500
So happens that when you sample
a lot from your own model

687
00:28:33,500 --> 00:28:35,140
and train on it on
policy, like, you

688
00:28:35,140 --> 00:28:36,980
sample from your
policy and train

689
00:28:36,980 --> 00:28:38,660
on it, you'll often
find that there

690
00:28:38,660 --> 00:28:42,420
are some of these traces which
end up having steps which

691
00:28:42,420 --> 00:28:45,480
are spurious in nature, steps
which actually are incorrect,

692
00:28:45,480 --> 00:28:48,480
but they just somehow don't
affect the model's generation.

693
00:28:48,480 --> 00:28:50,355
So I'm going to
show you an example.

694
00:28:50,355 --> 00:28:52,980
But basically, these are steps
which would take you to a regime

695
00:28:52,980 --> 00:28:55,620
where you're maybe doing
a wrong calculation.

696
00:28:55,620 --> 00:28:57,920
Somehow, you get there.

697
00:28:57,920 --> 00:28:59,580
Maybe on this
particular trace when

698
00:28:59,580 --> 00:29:01,100
you're sampling
from your model, you

699
00:29:01,100 --> 00:29:02,920
end up do recovering
from these traces.

700
00:29:02,920 --> 00:29:04,580
So you end up with
the correct answer.

701
00:29:04,580 --> 00:29:08,460
But maybe in reality, these
traces are actually harmful.

702
00:29:08,460 --> 00:29:10,300
So just to give you
an example, let's say

703
00:29:10,300 --> 00:29:12,520
this is a little
arithmetic problem.

704
00:29:12,520 --> 00:29:14,320
It's from this GSM8K data set.

705
00:29:14,320 --> 00:29:15,720
One interesting
example we found,

706
00:29:15,720 --> 00:29:18,700
which was the most stark example
of this particular issue,

707
00:29:18,700 --> 00:29:21,460
was there's a solution
from the model which

708
00:29:21,460 --> 00:29:22,900
does some calculation.

709
00:29:22,900 --> 00:29:26,500
Then it says that the total
amount of money needed

710
00:29:26,500 --> 00:29:28,840
is like, 100 times
2, which is 200,

711
00:29:28,840 --> 00:29:31,320
and it reverses its
calculation in the next line.

712
00:29:31,320 --> 00:29:35,520
So the true calculation is 100
by 2, but it ends up saying 200

713
00:29:35,520 --> 00:29:37,610
and then goes to
100 by 2 right away.

714
00:29:37,610 --> 00:29:39,860
So what ends up happening
is this particular step that

715
00:29:39,860 --> 00:29:42,920
exists in the middle, where it's
doing 200, is a spurious step.

716
00:29:42,920 --> 00:29:44,680
It's a step that is not needed.

717
00:29:44,680 --> 00:29:47,000
It's an incorrect step that
exists in the solution.

718
00:29:47,000 --> 00:29:48,500
You end up still
at the right answer

719
00:29:48,500 --> 00:29:50,860
in this particular
rollout, but this step

720
00:29:50,860 --> 00:29:55,020
shouldn't be there in reality
in that particular setting.

721
00:29:55,020 --> 00:29:58,278
So that's a spurious step
in this particular set.

722
00:29:58,278 --> 00:30:00,320
So the question is the
step is always a sentence.

723
00:30:00,320 --> 00:30:02,140
For now, you can
imagine it's always

724
00:30:02,140 --> 00:30:04,100
a sentence in this
particular setting.

725
00:30:04,100 --> 00:30:06,800
There's no universally
agreed upon definition to it.

726
00:30:06,800 --> 00:30:08,137
So this is a very--

727
00:30:08,137 --> 00:30:09,720
I guess in the second
part of my talk,

728
00:30:09,720 --> 00:30:11,540
I'm going to show you some
examples where the step is not

729
00:30:11,540 --> 00:30:12,320
at all a sentence.

730
00:30:12,320 --> 00:30:13,487
It's a full block of things.

731
00:30:13,487 --> 00:30:15,400
So yeah, that's
a great question.

732
00:30:15,400 --> 00:30:20,080
So the question is, "Is this the
main cause of increase in error?

733
00:30:20,080 --> 00:30:22,290
And can you not measure
it in some way?"

734
00:30:22,290 --> 00:30:24,090
So it turns out that
the answer to both

735
00:30:24,090 --> 00:30:27,290
is, yes, it's one of the main
causes for increase in error.

736
00:30:27,290 --> 00:30:29,330
Can you measure it in some way?

737
00:30:29,330 --> 00:30:31,410
At least normal notions
of fitting the data

738
00:30:31,410 --> 00:30:33,150
won't tell you this.

739
00:30:33,150 --> 00:30:34,150
That's a great question.

740
00:30:34,150 --> 00:30:35,850
So the question is, "Can we
associate the overfitting

741
00:30:35,850 --> 00:30:36,670
problem to this?"

742
00:30:36,670 --> 00:30:37,750
So you can actually
measure this.

743
00:30:37,750 --> 00:30:39,110
In the paper, we
did measure this.

744
00:30:39,110 --> 00:30:41,360
We measured the amount of
spurious steps that you see,

745
00:30:41,360 --> 00:30:42,443
and it grows on over time.

746
00:30:42,443 --> 00:30:44,860
Obviously, I cannot give you
a proof that this is the only

747
00:30:44,860 --> 00:30:45,370
cause.

748
00:30:45,370 --> 00:30:47,030
I can give you proof that
this should be a problem.

749
00:30:47,030 --> 00:30:49,363
I cannot give you a proof
that this is the only problem,

750
00:30:49,363 --> 00:30:51,632
but this is one of the
big problems that you see.

751
00:30:51,632 --> 00:30:54,090
So the question is, "How do
you find out if it's a spurious

752
00:30:54,090 --> 00:30:54,430
step?"

753
00:30:54,430 --> 00:30:56,472
In our experiments, we do
it in a particular way,

754
00:30:56,472 --> 00:30:58,370
and that also dictates
a lot of methodologies

755
00:30:58,370 --> 00:31:00,650
that I'm going to
show you in a second.

756
00:31:00,650 --> 00:31:02,217
So this particular method--

757
00:31:02,217 --> 00:31:04,550
so the question is, "Do you
sample entire trajectories?"

758
00:31:04,550 --> 00:31:07,050
This particular method
samples entire trajectories.

759
00:31:07,050 --> 00:31:09,530
You can sample a
parts of trajectories

760
00:31:09,530 --> 00:31:10,970
if you want it to
be, but I think

761
00:31:10,970 --> 00:31:13,330
it needs some advanced
machinery that I

762
00:31:13,330 --> 00:31:15,850
think can be done based on
what I'm going to talk about

763
00:31:15,850 --> 00:31:17,350
in the next slide.

764
00:31:17,350 --> 00:31:18,350
That's a great question.

765
00:31:18,350 --> 00:31:20,370
So the question is, "Do you
overfit to a certain class

766
00:31:20,370 --> 00:31:22,210
of methods that got
to the right answer?"

767
00:31:22,210 --> 00:31:26,450
In a way, the answer is, at a
high level, yes, with the caveat

768
00:31:26,450 --> 00:31:29,170
that I don't know how to
define what a method means.

769
00:31:29,170 --> 00:31:31,470
So you can call a method that--

770
00:31:31,470 --> 00:31:33,290
so just seeing this
example, one can

771
00:31:33,290 --> 00:31:36,170
say that the calculation
of 100 times 2

772
00:31:36,170 --> 00:31:38,530
is a methodological phenomenon
that the model wants

773
00:31:38,530 --> 00:31:40,490
to do to be able to
then succeed and then

774
00:31:40,490 --> 00:31:42,290
realize that it was wrong.

775
00:31:42,290 --> 00:31:44,490
You can also say that
really, the method here

776
00:31:44,490 --> 00:31:46,835
is nothing but just doing
a bunch of arithmetic,

777
00:31:46,835 --> 00:31:48,710
so it really depends on
what you define that.

778
00:31:48,710 --> 00:31:51,110
And I think it's a semantic
question at that point.

779
00:31:51,110 --> 00:31:54,103
But yeah, so from a very
symbolic perspective,

780
00:31:54,103 --> 00:31:56,770
one can say that there are a set
of tokens that maybe still lead

781
00:31:56,770 --> 00:31:58,742
you to the right answer
on the training set,

782
00:31:58,742 --> 00:32:01,450
but may not actually help you do
the right thing on the test set,

783
00:32:01,450 --> 00:32:03,170
on a test problem.

784
00:32:03,170 --> 00:32:06,290
So this is very similar to the
problem of causal confusion

785
00:32:06,290 --> 00:32:08,810
in imitation learning actually.

786
00:32:08,810 --> 00:32:10,750
I'm not sure if
causal confusion--

787
00:32:10,750 --> 00:32:12,153
OK, OK, OK, never mind.

788
00:32:12,153 --> 00:32:13,570
But basically
there's this problem

789
00:32:13,570 --> 00:32:17,930
of if you produce
a bunch of stuff

790
00:32:17,930 --> 00:32:23,220
in your policy, that might be
bad, like these spurious steps,

791
00:32:23,220 --> 00:32:25,120
you might just not
learn to imitate

792
00:32:25,120 --> 00:32:26,900
the expert in the
right way possible.

793
00:32:26,900 --> 00:32:30,858
So maybe you actually end
up at the right answer,

794
00:32:30,858 --> 00:32:32,900
but you're learning these
incorrect associations.

795
00:32:32,900 --> 00:32:35,442
That's basically the gist of
this problem of causal confusion

796
00:32:35,442 --> 00:32:36,880
that people in
imitation learning

797
00:32:36,880 --> 00:32:40,040
know a lot about actually,

798
00:32:40,040 --> 00:32:41,493
OK, so how can we address this?

799
00:32:41,493 --> 00:32:43,160
And that's where RL
comes in, and that's

800
00:32:43,160 --> 00:32:45,400
the third axis of
using incorrect data

801
00:32:45,400 --> 00:32:47,720
or incorrect rollouts
for learning.

802
00:32:47,720 --> 00:32:50,080
The simple idea
is, as many of you

803
00:32:50,080 --> 00:32:52,920
already asked questions
about, assigning some notion

804
00:32:52,920 --> 00:32:54,260
of a credit to a step.

805
00:32:54,260 --> 00:32:58,760
So if I can understand what is
the utility of every single step

806
00:32:58,760 --> 00:33:01,340
or every single state
that my policy generates,

807
00:33:01,340 --> 00:33:03,160
then I can somehow
use that notion

808
00:33:03,160 --> 00:33:08,160
to filter off these bad kinds
of solutions in some way.

809
00:33:08,160 --> 00:33:11,640
So that's exactly what we
do, or what RL for reasoning

810
00:33:11,640 --> 00:33:13,680
tries to do at a
high level as well.

811
00:33:13,680 --> 00:33:15,880
So identifying
these spurious steps

812
00:33:15,880 --> 00:33:18,240
and eliminating
them in some way.

813
00:33:18,240 --> 00:33:21,520
So the way to do this is the
following-- and this ties

814
00:33:21,520 --> 00:33:24,000
back to a lot of concepts of
value functions and advantage

815
00:33:24,000 --> 00:33:27,028
functions that many of you
are probably familiar with.

816
00:33:27,028 --> 00:33:28,820
So I'm going to start
with an illustration,

817
00:33:28,820 --> 00:33:31,800
and then I'll write down the
exact math for what this means.

818
00:33:31,800 --> 00:33:33,720
So let's say I gave you
a particular rollout

819
00:33:33,720 --> 00:33:35,200
or a particular trace.

820
00:33:35,200 --> 00:33:36,800
So again, starting
from a prompt,

821
00:33:36,800 --> 00:33:39,287
adding these steps
or actions and going

822
00:33:39,287 --> 00:33:41,620
to a final answer, which maybe
ended up being incorrect,

823
00:33:41,620 --> 00:33:43,640
so it's shown as a red dot.

824
00:33:43,640 --> 00:33:47,120
What I can do now is I can try
to identify whether a step is

825
00:33:47,120 --> 00:33:51,040
spurious or not by taking
these particular prefixes,

826
00:33:51,040 --> 00:33:53,360
or taking these particular
states that were basically

827
00:33:53,360 --> 00:33:55,400
concatenating all
the tokens so far,

828
00:33:55,400 --> 00:33:59,400
and doing what you can think
of as rollouts in a way.

829
00:33:59,400 --> 00:34:03,680
So I can ask some model to try
to complete the solution, given

830
00:34:03,680 --> 00:34:06,240
a prefix of a
generated solution.

831
00:34:06,240 --> 00:34:08,230
So for example, in
this particular setting

832
00:34:08,230 --> 00:34:10,480
that you see on the slide,
I have kept the first three

833
00:34:10,480 --> 00:34:11,920
steps the same.

834
00:34:11,920 --> 00:34:15,440
And I'm asking a separate model
or a separate rollout policy

835
00:34:15,440 --> 00:34:18,500
to try to complete the solution,
given this particular prefix.

836
00:34:18,500 --> 00:34:21,250

837
00:34:21,250 --> 00:34:23,969
So if I do this, and let's say
the rollout ends up succeeding--

838
00:34:23,969 --> 00:34:26,830
so many of these rollouts
end up at the right answer--

839
00:34:26,830 --> 00:34:30,150
then, likely, the prefix was
not too bad because, clearly,

840
00:34:30,150 --> 00:34:32,370
for this particular
rollout policy,

841
00:34:32,370 --> 00:34:35,790
it did not end up
affecting any of

842
00:34:35,790 --> 00:34:38,920
its problem-solving abilities.

843
00:34:38,920 --> 00:34:40,670
On the other hand,
let's say if I ended up

844
00:34:40,670 --> 00:34:43,750
at a totally wrong answer,
conditioned on the prefix

845
00:34:43,750 --> 00:34:47,889
that you see up here, clearly,
something is not OK here.

846
00:34:47,889 --> 00:34:50,592
And clearly, there's something
wrong with the prefix

847
00:34:50,592 --> 00:34:52,550
that you're seeing up
here because your rollout

848
00:34:52,550 --> 00:34:55,590
policy could not at all succeed,
conditioned on that prefix, even

849
00:34:55,590 --> 00:34:58,430
after doing many
possible rollouts.

850
00:34:58,430 --> 00:35:01,050
And if I ended up succeeding
sometimes and sometimes not,

851
00:35:01,050 --> 00:35:02,490
maybe there's still some issue.

852
00:35:02,490 --> 00:35:04,282
Maybe it's not the best
thing you can have,

853
00:35:04,282 --> 00:35:06,230
but at least it's
slightly better

854
00:35:06,230 --> 00:35:10,670
than always failing that you
see up here, for instance.

855
00:35:10,670 --> 00:35:13,230
So this basic principle of
doing rollouts and estimating

856
00:35:13,230 --> 00:35:15,990
some notion of expected
success in the future

857
00:35:15,990 --> 00:35:17,910
can tell you
something about what

858
00:35:17,910 --> 00:35:20,670
is wrong with the current
prefix that you are particularly

859
00:35:20,670 --> 00:35:22,670
operating at.

860
00:35:22,670 --> 00:35:27,030
This is also very similar to a
value function because this is

861
00:35:27,030 --> 00:35:31,870
basically computing the expected
future 01 reward that my rollout

862
00:35:31,870 --> 00:35:34,950
policy, that this orange policy
gets when it acts in this

863
00:35:34,950 --> 00:35:36,910
particular MDP.

864
00:35:36,910 --> 00:35:39,710
So the question is-- the
spurious step on the previous

865
00:35:39,710 --> 00:35:40,510
slide--

866
00:35:40,510 --> 00:35:45,950
"IS that actually
irrelevant or Incorrect?"

867
00:35:45,950 --> 00:35:47,870
Or irrelevant slash--

868
00:35:47,870 --> 00:35:49,630
I mean, it's incorrect
purely because--

869
00:35:49,630 --> 00:35:52,970
or OK, the step is not
incorrect in its own right,

870
00:35:52,970 --> 00:35:55,550
but it is irrelevant a way.

871
00:35:55,550 --> 00:35:57,710
Imagine for a second that
if you pass to a language

872
00:35:57,710 --> 00:36:00,670
model a million
irrelevant tokens

873
00:36:00,670 --> 00:36:03,270
and ask it to dig out the
information that actually

874
00:36:03,270 --> 00:36:05,590
matters, that can be a
challenging problem on a test

875
00:36:05,590 --> 00:36:06,090
set.

876
00:36:06,090 --> 00:36:08,550
So that's why, even if
a step is irrelevant,

877
00:36:08,550 --> 00:36:11,130
it can be a problem
for the model.

878
00:36:11,130 --> 00:36:11,630
OK.

879
00:36:11,630 --> 00:36:13,010
So we did these rollouts.

880
00:36:13,010 --> 00:36:16,245
We computed the expected value
of how many times can I succeed?

881
00:36:16,245 --> 00:36:17,870
And that resembles
something that looks

882
00:36:17,870 --> 00:36:19,998
like a value function of sorts.

883
00:36:19,998 --> 00:36:21,290
And why is it a value function?

884
00:36:21,290 --> 00:36:24,735
Because if you write this
down in a bit of math--

885
00:36:24,735 --> 00:36:27,110
so let's say if I define the
problem or the initial state

886
00:36:27,110 --> 00:36:31,910
as x, each of these steps
as Y1's to Yn's So I

887
00:36:31,910 --> 00:36:34,230
guess there's a bit of a
notation mismatch here Y1

888
00:36:34,230 --> 00:36:36,190
to Yn's are independent rollout.

889
00:36:36,190 --> 00:36:38,670
Here, the Yi's I'm showing
are basically each step

890
00:36:38,670 --> 00:36:41,030
or each action that you take.

891
00:36:41,030 --> 00:36:45,910
So if you define a quantity
that looks like the following,

892
00:36:45,910 --> 00:36:47,290
it's basically a Q function.

893
00:36:47,290 --> 00:36:48,470
So what is this quantity?

894
00:36:48,470 --> 00:36:52,670
It's telling you the Q value
for your particular rollout

895
00:36:52,670 --> 00:36:54,130
policy, pi tilde.

896
00:36:54,130 --> 00:36:56,790
So the policy which is trying
to complete the solution,

897
00:36:56,790 --> 00:36:58,350
rollout policy pi tilde.

898
00:36:58,350 --> 00:37:02,990
I'm computing its Q value at a
given state given by the initial

899
00:37:02,990 --> 00:37:05,510
state and the prefix
of the solution so far,

900
00:37:05,510 --> 00:37:07,870
and given an action, which
is the current step that

901
00:37:07,870 --> 00:37:12,220
your policy adds, I can define
the Q value here as the expected

902
00:37:12,220 --> 00:37:16,060
long-term reward, meaning
the expected 01 reward,

903
00:37:16,060 --> 00:37:19,140
that I get under
my rollout policy.

904
00:37:19,140 --> 00:37:22,492
If I start by executing--

905
00:37:22,492 --> 00:37:25,860
if I start by getting
actions I plus 1 to L

906
00:37:25,860 --> 00:37:27,360
from this rollout policy.

907
00:37:27,360 --> 00:37:28,960
So what's the Q function?

908
00:37:28,960 --> 00:37:31,680
A Q function, just
to recall, Q pi--

909
00:37:31,680 --> 00:37:34,180
I guess, it's subscript pi.

910
00:37:34,180 --> 00:37:42,180
Q pi of s,a is basically nothing
but R of s,a, or, I guess,

911
00:37:42,180 --> 00:37:44,160
depends upon how you write this.

912
00:37:44,160 --> 00:37:53,260
But it's basically the expected
discounted reward that you get,

913
00:37:53,260 --> 00:37:56,500
starting from P equal to
0 to, let's say, infinity

914
00:37:56,500 --> 00:37:59,820
in general, conditioned
on S0 being S

915
00:37:59,820 --> 00:38:03,603
and A0 being A. This is the
definition of a Q function.

916
00:38:03,603 --> 00:38:05,020
In this particular
setting, we are

917
00:38:05,020 --> 00:38:07,000
assuming discount factor is 1.

918
00:38:07,000 --> 00:38:08,980
So gamma is set to exactly 1.

919
00:38:08,980 --> 00:38:12,120
And the reward that you
get is only at termination,

920
00:38:12,120 --> 00:38:16,120
so there's 0000 until you end
up hitting the final answer.

921
00:38:16,120 --> 00:38:18,620
So that's why you see there's
only one expectation up there.

922
00:38:18,620 --> 00:38:21,800
And this expectation is
computed under policy pi.

923
00:38:21,800 --> 00:38:27,100
So you roll this policy
pi out from there on.

924
00:38:27,100 --> 00:38:28,560
There's one minor detail here.

925
00:38:28,560 --> 00:38:31,927
You typically have R of s,a plus
this thing because you roll out

926
00:38:31,927 --> 00:38:34,260
the policy from the next step,
and you sum up the reward

927
00:38:34,260 --> 00:38:35,255
at the current state.

928
00:38:35,255 --> 00:38:37,880
In this case, it doesn't matter
because r of s,a is equal to 0.

929
00:38:37,880 --> 00:38:39,260
You only get a
reward at the end.

930
00:38:39,260 --> 00:38:40,583
Gamma is set to 1.

931
00:38:40,583 --> 00:38:42,500
So essentially, the thing
that you see up here

932
00:38:42,500 --> 00:38:45,140
is essentially an
expected future reward,

933
00:38:45,140 --> 00:38:46,940
starting from a
given prefix, which

934
00:38:46,940 --> 00:38:50,700
is the state at a given
step, which is the action.

935
00:38:50,700 --> 00:38:54,420
And the rollout is
performed under pi tilde

936
00:38:54,420 --> 00:38:56,180
that you see up here.

937
00:38:56,180 --> 00:38:59,140
So the question is, "Would you
use the same policy to roll out

938
00:38:59,140 --> 00:39:00,860
those other trajectories?"

939
00:39:00,860 --> 00:39:03,140
So in principle, this
equation that I wrote

940
00:39:03,140 --> 00:39:08,040
is agnostic of which policy
you use to do the rollout.

941
00:39:08,040 --> 00:39:10,810
So there's been actually no--

942
00:39:10,810 --> 00:39:13,370
so I have a belief
about this answer,

943
00:39:13,370 --> 00:39:15,878
about this particular
choice of the policy.

944
00:39:15,878 --> 00:39:18,170
So in my opinion, the policy
that you're doing rollouts

945
00:39:18,170 --> 00:39:19,690
with should be a
different policy,

946
00:39:19,690 --> 00:39:21,440
compared to the current
one, otherwise, it

947
00:39:21,440 --> 00:39:22,668
can fail in the same way.

948
00:39:22,668 --> 00:39:24,210
There's some work
in literature which

949
00:39:24,210 --> 00:39:26,330
argues that even
using the same policy

950
00:39:26,330 --> 00:39:28,810
can actually be good enough.

951
00:39:28,810 --> 00:39:32,010
I think we haven't seen an
apples-to-apples comparison,

952
00:39:32,010 --> 00:39:34,730
given the scale of
experiments, which definitively

953
00:39:34,730 --> 00:39:36,475
shows one way or the other.

954
00:39:36,475 --> 00:39:38,850
I think most of the evidence
that exists right now points

955
00:39:38,850 --> 00:39:41,150
