So that's the first option.

1225
00:50:50,398 --> 00:50:52,440
So this is a lot like
filtered imitation learning

1226
00:50:52,440 --> 00:50:54,967
or advantage-weighted
regression.

1227
00:50:54,967 --> 00:50:57,050
OK, before that, I can
show you some results here.

1228
00:50:57,050 --> 00:51:00,190
So this actually ended up
addressing that error going up

1229
00:51:00,190 --> 00:51:01,870
problem, at least to
the extent that we

1230
00:51:01,870 --> 00:51:03,310
were able to train models.

1231
00:51:03,310 --> 00:51:06,710
So here, again, I'm plotting
the number of correct answers

1232
00:51:06,710 --> 00:51:07,450
per question.

1233
00:51:07,450 --> 00:51:10,630
And you find that the advantage
filtering did help you,

1234
00:51:10,630 --> 00:51:15,350
compared to the RFP approach
which was going up before.

1235
00:51:15,350 --> 00:51:17,807
So it does actually--
and we did measure

1236
00:51:17,807 --> 00:51:19,390
the number of spurious
steps according

1237
00:51:19,390 --> 00:51:22,490
to some heuristic definition
of how we can evaluate this,

1238
00:51:22,490 --> 00:51:23,570
how can probe this.

1239
00:51:23,570 --> 00:51:26,550
And it seemed like, just from
a qualitative perspective also,

1240
00:51:26,550 --> 00:51:32,270
the number of spurious steps
actually reduced in some way.

1241
00:51:32,270 --> 00:51:32,950
So

1242
00:51:32,950 --> 00:51:34,030
The next thing is,
how can you use

1243
00:51:34,030 --> 00:51:35,730
this for offline
reinforcement learning?

1244
00:51:35,730 --> 00:51:39,010
And this is where
DPO-based methods come in.

1245
00:51:39,010 --> 00:51:41,610
So there was a question about
this earlier in the class.

1246
00:51:41,610 --> 00:51:42,990
So you can also use them for--

1247
00:51:42,990 --> 00:51:44,448
you can also use
the same principle

1248
00:51:44,448 --> 00:51:46,110
for doing DPO actually.

1249
00:51:46,110 --> 00:51:50,030
So the idea in DPO is slightly
different from computing

1250
00:51:50,030 --> 00:51:52,390
this advantage exactly,
but uses the same protocol

1251
00:51:52,390 --> 00:51:54,150
that I described to
you earlier, where

1252
00:51:54,150 --> 00:51:55,692
what you're going
to do now is you're

1253
00:51:55,692 --> 00:51:57,990
going to take a solution
from your base model

1254
00:51:57,990 --> 00:51:59,710
that you're training.

1255
00:51:59,710 --> 00:52:01,165
Maybe this is incorrect.

1256
00:52:01,165 --> 00:52:03,790
And so far, what we did was we
found out steps that were useful

1257
00:52:03,790 --> 00:52:06,123
and retained them in some way
or the other and discarded

1258
00:52:06,123 --> 00:52:06,810
everything else.

1259
00:52:06,810 --> 00:52:09,670
But what we can do now is
we can do these rollouts.

1260
00:52:09,670 --> 00:52:14,790
And rather than throwing away
or throwing away these rollouts

1261
00:52:14,790 --> 00:52:17,670
or just using these rollouts
to estimate the advantage, what

1262
00:52:17,670 --> 00:52:20,990
I can do is I can, instead,
take a rollout, which ended up

1263
00:52:20,990 --> 00:52:24,230
succeeding, with
a shared prefix;

1264
00:52:24,230 --> 00:52:26,430
take the solution trace
from my model, which did not

1265
00:52:26,430 --> 00:52:30,550
end up succeeding, and construct
a preference pair out of them.

1266
00:52:30,550 --> 00:52:34,910
So I now have a preference pair,
which looks like some prefix,

1267
00:52:34,910 --> 00:52:37,430
followed by some
correction, which is good,

1268
00:52:37,430 --> 00:52:41,230
and some prefix followed by some
steps which are actually wrong.

1269
00:52:41,230 --> 00:52:45,150
And I can throw this into a DPR
training data set in some way.

1270
00:52:45,150 --> 00:52:48,610
I can also do the same thing
with a correct rollout.

1271
00:52:48,610 --> 00:52:51,660
So let's say I have a correct
rollout from my base model.

1272
00:52:51,660 --> 00:52:54,300
I can still do these rollouts,
and maybe one of them

1273
00:52:54,300 --> 00:52:57,020
will not end up
succeeding potentially

1274
00:52:57,020 --> 00:52:58,040
from my rollout policy.

1275
00:52:58,040 --> 00:53:01,060
And I can basically put this
thing as a preference pair

1276
00:53:01,060 --> 00:53:03,623
into my training mix.

1277
00:53:03,623 --> 00:53:05,540
So the nice thing that
is happening-- or there

1278
00:53:05,540 --> 00:53:07,373
are two differences
that are happening here.

1279
00:53:07,373 --> 00:53:10,140
In some way, I'm still
realizing in this process

1280
00:53:10,140 --> 00:53:12,080
that prefixes are shared.

1281
00:53:12,080 --> 00:53:14,248
And really, the difference
between a correct

1282
00:53:14,248 --> 00:53:16,540
and an incorrect rollout
comes from that little segment

1283
00:53:16,540 --> 00:53:20,762
that you see, beyond the
prefix in these settings.

1284
00:53:20,762 --> 00:53:21,720
That's the first thing.

1285
00:53:21,720 --> 00:53:22,595
And the second thing.

1286
00:53:22,595 --> 00:53:24,380
Is, rather than the
previous approach

1287
00:53:24,380 --> 00:53:26,940
of filtering by
advantages, I'm actually

1288
00:53:26,940 --> 00:53:28,940
retaining the whole
steps that appear

1289
00:53:28,940 --> 00:53:31,260
in this rollout distribution.

1290
00:53:31,260 --> 00:53:33,420
So I'm not simply
using these rollouts

1291
00:53:33,420 --> 00:53:37,440
like the orange-colored, dashed
lines to compute advantages,

1292
00:53:37,440 --> 00:53:39,180
but I'm retaining
them, and I'm training

1293
00:53:39,180 --> 00:53:42,940
on top of the tokens that
appear in that rollout as well.

1294
00:53:42,940 --> 00:53:46,080
So the DPO loss, I guess, I
don't have that on my slides.

1295
00:53:46,080 --> 00:53:48,860
But, I guess
probably I don't need

1296
00:53:48,860 --> 00:53:50,400
to necessarily write that down.

1297
00:53:50,400 --> 00:53:53,220
But it's basically just a
matter of the same old DPO

1298
00:53:53,220 --> 00:53:56,980
loss on top of these two
segments of solutions

1299
00:53:56,980 --> 00:53:59,300
that we talked about.

1300
00:53:59,300 --> 00:54:00,372
Yeah, great question.

1301
00:54:00,372 --> 00:54:02,580
So the question is, "Do you
want higher-quality text,

1302
00:54:02,580 --> 00:54:05,420
or do you want more diversity?"

1303
00:54:05,420 --> 00:54:08,060
I think the true--

1304
00:54:08,060 --> 00:54:11,740
OK, I should mention that
this is something that we

1305
00:54:11,740 --> 00:54:13,000
have tried to formalize.

1306
00:54:13,000 --> 00:54:15,660
So there's an exact equation
that you can write down.

1307
00:54:15,660 --> 00:54:18,100
And you can be like OK,
I want a policy to do

1308
00:54:18,100 --> 00:54:21,940
these rollouts which maximizes
this expression sort of thing

1309
00:54:21,940 --> 00:54:24,500
in one of the papers.

1310
00:54:24,500 --> 00:54:27,500
I think that kind of balances,
in some way, diversity

1311
00:54:27,500 --> 00:54:28,980
and high quality.

1312
00:54:28,980 --> 00:54:31,760
So imagine for a second that
your model is really bad.

1313
00:54:31,760 --> 00:54:35,440
So let's say your model mostly
produces incorrect traces.

1314
00:54:35,440 --> 00:54:37,907
Then you want a rollout
policy that can actually

1315
00:54:37,907 --> 00:54:39,740
somewhat succeed because,
otherwise, there's

1316
00:54:39,740 --> 00:54:41,907
no notion of a success that
you can learn from ever.

1317
00:54:41,907 --> 00:54:43,192
You've only seen failures.

1318
00:54:43,192 --> 00:54:45,400
How can you actually even
learn to solve that problem

1319
00:54:45,400 --> 00:54:46,818
if you only see failures?

1320
00:54:46,818 --> 00:54:49,360
On the other hand, let's say if
your model is kind of decent,

1321
00:54:49,360 --> 00:54:52,320
like 50% accurate, but
it's not as good overall.

1322
00:54:52,320 --> 00:54:54,393
It's not the best
at solving problems.

1323
00:54:54,393 --> 00:54:56,060
In that case, maybe
all you want is just

1324
00:54:56,060 --> 00:54:57,480
diverse ways of
solving the problem,

1325
00:54:57,480 --> 00:55:00,022
such that you can see what ways
you're more prone to failing.

1326
00:55:00,022 --> 00:55:02,940
So you care about
coverage in some ways.

1327
00:55:02,940 --> 00:55:05,640
So really, the answer here is
like every single problem in RL,

1328
00:55:05,640 --> 00:55:08,057
there is some balance between
exploration and exploitation

1329
00:55:08,057 --> 00:55:10,060
you want to do, which
is high-quality text

1330
00:55:10,060 --> 00:55:12,760
or high-quality solutions, which
solve the question correctly

1331
00:55:12,760 --> 00:55:17,318
and being more
diverse in some way.

1332
00:55:17,318 --> 00:55:18,860
I think the exact
expression for this

1333
00:55:18,860 --> 00:55:21,380
is actually something
you can also write down.

1334
00:55:21,380 --> 00:55:23,160
And I'm not going
to cover today,

1335
00:55:23,160 --> 00:55:25,820
but, yeah, it's a
trade-off between them.

1336
00:55:25,820 --> 00:55:28,460
Yeah, so basically
used to train DPO.

1337
00:55:28,460 --> 00:55:30,695
So far, I guess--

1338
00:55:30,695 --> 00:55:32,820
OK, actually, let me just
write that algorithm down

1339
00:55:32,820 --> 00:55:35,500
for everybody's understanding.

1340
00:55:35,500 --> 00:55:37,620
So what you do is you're
basically-- so that

1341
00:55:37,620 --> 00:55:39,100
is the RFD algorithm.

1342
00:55:39,100 --> 00:55:41,020
And now what we're
going to do is

1343
00:55:41,020 --> 00:55:42,800
we're going to simply
do the same process.

1344
00:55:42,800 --> 00:55:52,410
So for iteration in 1 to
T, I'm going to sample Y1

1345
00:55:52,410 --> 00:55:57,450
to N from my current
policy, given any question.

1346
00:55:57,450 --> 00:55:59,350
I am going to now
do these rollouts,

1347
00:55:59,350 --> 00:56:06,730
so I'm going to do rollouts
and construct the preference

1348
00:56:06,730 --> 00:56:08,210
data set Dpref.

1349
00:56:08,210 --> 00:56:11,030
In this case, it's
basically those preferences.

1350
00:56:11,030 --> 00:56:13,090
And now what I'm going
to do is I'm going

1351
00:56:13,090 --> 00:56:15,640
to run or minimize the loss.

1352
00:56:15,640 --> 00:56:23,850

1353
00:56:23,850 --> 00:56:26,250
That's the main algorithm
that you're going to do.

1354
00:56:26,250 --> 00:56:29,770
And the Dpref reef consists
of prefixes generated

1355
00:56:29,770 --> 00:56:31,330
from your current base model.

1356
00:56:31,330 --> 00:56:33,650
And the trace is
coming from-- one part

1357
00:56:33,650 --> 00:56:36,517
of it is from the same model
that you are training pi theta.

1358
00:56:36,517 --> 00:56:38,850
The other segment, the other
part of the preference pair

1359
00:56:38,850 --> 00:56:41,210
comes from the rollout policy.

1360
00:56:41,210 --> 00:56:42,632
That's the orange line.

1361
00:56:42,632 --> 00:56:44,590
So the question is, "Given
a sample trajectory,

1362
00:56:44,590 --> 00:56:46,070
how we segment into steps?"

1363
00:56:46,070 --> 00:56:48,128
So in this work, we
just used sentences.

1364
00:56:48,128 --> 00:56:49,670
So we just had
[INAUDIBLE] sentences.

1365
00:56:49,670 --> 00:56:51,378
We're going to break
them down into steps

1366
00:56:51,378 --> 00:56:52,970
from after every sentence.

1367
00:56:52,970 --> 00:56:54,952
In general, I think it depends--

1368
00:56:54,952 --> 00:56:56,910
it's a thing that you
can bake into your model.

1369
00:56:56,910 --> 00:57:00,417
So if you have played with,
for example, OpenAI models,

1370
00:57:00,417 --> 00:57:02,250
you'll see that they
often produce responses

1371
00:57:02,250 --> 00:57:06,430
that look like step one in
markdown, like a very big step

1372
00:57:06,430 --> 00:57:08,930
one, and then they'll say a
bunch of stuff and then step two

1373
00:57:08,930 --> 00:57:12,290
and then say a bunch of stuff
so you can use that notation.

1374
00:57:12,290 --> 00:57:15,890
Oftentimes it's so happening
that as these models get better

1375
00:57:15,890 --> 00:57:17,650
at basic instruction
following, you

1376
00:57:17,650 --> 00:57:20,170
can just prompt them
to produce responses

1377
00:57:20,170 --> 00:57:23,250
for complex questions in a
particular stylistic fashion,

1378
00:57:23,250 --> 00:57:25,410
and use some heuristics
about that style

1379
00:57:25,410 --> 00:57:26,998
to partition things into steps.

1380
00:57:26,998 --> 00:57:28,790
So for instance, you
can just tell a model,

1381
00:57:28,790 --> 00:57:33,970
well, I want an answer for this
particular question that never

1382
00:57:33,970 --> 00:57:37,770
uses the word step,
that always produces

1383
00:57:37,770 --> 00:57:41,650
a logical block of calculation,
puts a horizontal line

1384
00:57:41,650 --> 00:57:43,830
and then pushes the next
logical block and so on.

1385
00:57:43,830 --> 00:57:45,663
And it'll most
likely do it for you,

1386
00:57:45,663 --> 00:57:47,080
which is something
you can also do

1387
00:57:47,080 --> 00:57:48,100
during the course of training.

1388
00:57:48,100 --> 00:57:49,840
So you can just prompt
them to produce solutions

1389
00:57:49,840 --> 00:57:52,200
in a particular way and then
define steps accordingly.

1390
00:57:52,200 --> 00:57:53,898
There's no universal definition.

1391
00:57:53,898 --> 00:57:56,440
So the question is, "Is there
a convention to produce special

1392
00:57:56,440 --> 00:57:58,840
tokens for generating steps?"

1393
00:57:58,840 --> 00:58:01,880
I think that I've not seen a
very, very clear convention that

1394
00:58:01,880 --> 00:58:03,977
is universally
used all the time.

1395
00:58:03,977 --> 00:58:05,560
One convention that
we have been using

1396
00:58:05,560 --> 00:58:07,102
that I've seen many
other papers use,

1397
00:58:07,102 --> 00:58:08,800
but they're still
outliers for this,

1398
00:58:08,800 --> 00:58:13,220
is they would produce step hash
1 colon and then say something.

1399
00:58:13,220 --> 00:58:15,440
And that could be a
bunch of lines as well.

1400
00:58:15,440 --> 00:58:17,520
In this work, we use lines.

1401
00:58:17,520 --> 00:58:20,420
In a follow-up thing, we
had used this step notion.

1402
00:58:20,420 --> 00:58:23,260
So it really depends on up
to you, really, I think.

1403
00:58:23,260 --> 00:58:25,240
Yeah.

1404
00:58:25,240 --> 00:58:26,260
OK, cool.

1405
00:58:26,260 --> 00:58:28,660
Any questions on the
DPO part in this case?

1406
00:58:28,660 --> 00:58:31,540
So this is the DPO
version of the algorithm.

1407
00:58:31,540 --> 00:58:35,980

1408
00:58:35,980 --> 00:58:36,480
Great.

1409
00:58:36,480 --> 00:58:36,980
OK.

1410
00:58:36,980 --> 00:58:41,480
So you can do this and
put this into your data.

1411
00:58:41,480 --> 00:58:44,940
So if you go back to our plot
from before, if you measure how.

1412
00:58:44,940 --> 00:58:47,360
How well does such kind
of an offline RL method

1413
00:58:47,360 --> 00:58:49,280
do, what ends up happening is--

1414
00:58:49,280 --> 00:58:52,320
OK, I guess, these were supposed
to be GIFs, but anyways.

1415
00:58:52,320 --> 00:58:55,120
So I'm now plotting
the test error

1416
00:58:55,120 --> 00:58:57,040
as a function of
number of questions

1417
00:58:57,040 --> 00:58:59,985
that existed in my data set.

1418
00:58:59,985 --> 00:59:01,360
So far, we were
looking at number

1419
00:59:01,360 --> 00:59:03,520
of correct answers
per question for a lot

1420
00:59:03,520 --> 00:59:04,902
of the plots in the middle.

1421
00:59:04,902 --> 00:59:06,360
But now, I'm back
to the plot where

1422
00:59:06,360 --> 00:59:09,720
we are plotting the x-axis as a
function of number of questions

1423
00:59:09,720 --> 00:59:11,320
in the original data set.

1424
00:59:11,320 --> 00:59:13,040
And you find that, compared to--

1425
00:59:13,040 --> 00:59:14,500
and now there are
two models here.

1426
00:59:14,500 --> 00:59:16,180
One of them is a Llama 2 model.

1427
00:59:16,180 --> 00:59:18,320
So this probably it
tells you how old

1428
00:59:18,320 --> 00:59:20,040
this work is at this point.

1429
00:59:20,040 --> 00:59:24,160
It's a year old, but Llama 2
feels like a long time ago--

1430
00:59:24,160 --> 00:59:27,770
and then DeepSeek V2 models,
as well as DeepSeek V2 models.

1431
00:59:27,770 --> 00:59:29,520
And what we're finding
in general is that,

1432
00:59:29,520 --> 00:59:32,480
compared to the base SFT method,
where you were just training

1433
00:59:32,480 --> 00:59:36,880
on human-written solutions,
you can get up to an 8x boost

1434
00:59:36,880 --> 00:59:39,460
in the amount of data that
you need for training.

1435
00:59:39,460 --> 00:59:43,430
So the dashed line here is
this first-step BPO algorithm

1436
00:59:43,430 --> 00:59:44,790
that I talked about.

1437
00:59:44,790 --> 00:59:48,150
And you see that it can get
