1442
00:59:58,470 --> 01:00:01,830
But in general, this 8x also
holds true for Llama too roughly

1443
01:00:01,830 --> 01:00:03,790
overall in this setting.

1444
01:00:03,790 --> 01:00:05,690
Yeah, so the question
is, "What is Pass@5?"

1445
01:00:05,690 --> 01:00:07,950
That's a great question.

1446
01:00:07,950 --> 01:00:09,828
So you can think of Pass@5--

1447
01:00:09,828 --> 01:00:11,370
I guess, for
understanding the slide,

1448
01:00:11,370 --> 01:00:12,530
it doesn't matter first of all.

1449
01:00:12,530 --> 01:00:15,030
But in general, you can think
of Pass@5 basically as saying,

1450
01:00:15,030 --> 01:00:16,450
if I gave the
model five chances,

1451
01:00:16,450 --> 01:00:18,910
can it succeed even once
out of those five chances?

1452
01:00:18,910 --> 01:00:21,450
So it's a way people
evaluate models

1453
01:00:21,450 --> 01:00:23,950
because it's telling you that,
OK, maybe the model could not

1454
01:00:23,950 --> 01:00:26,575
succeed in one try, but
if I gave it n tries,

1455
01:00:26,575 --> 01:00:27,450
maybe it can succeed.

1456
01:00:27,450 --> 01:00:30,570
And that's what Pass@n
means in the setting.

1457
01:00:30,570 --> 01:00:32,690
I mean, I don't think for
this particular result,

1458
01:00:32,690 --> 01:00:33,830
it matters so much.

1459
01:00:33,830 --> 01:00:36,870
The main thing to look at is
