1460
01:00:36,870 --> 01:00:39,150
that you see up here.

1461
01:00:39,150 --> 01:00:40,830
So the high-level
takeaway here is

1462
01:00:40,830 --> 01:00:44,230
that if you do offline RL with
this first step notion of credit

1463
01:00:44,230 --> 01:00:48,470
assignment that we talked
about, you can actually

1464
01:00:48,470 --> 01:00:50,430
get a lot more data
efficiency boost

1465
01:00:50,430 --> 01:00:51,770
compared to imitation learning.

1466
01:00:51,770 --> 01:00:54,870
So for the same amount of
data, for the same base model--

1467
01:00:54,870 --> 01:00:57,950
or sorry, for the same
base model and for 8x fewer

1468
01:00:57,950 --> 01:01:00,150
questions, you
can do a similar--

1469
01:01:00,150 --> 01:01:03,550
you can get the same performance
with RL in this setting.

1470
01:01:03,550 --> 01:01:06,382
Yeah, so, generally,
I've seen people use 0.6

1471
01:01:06,382 --> 01:01:08,590
as the temperature for a
lot of the-- so the question

1472
01:01:08,590 --> 01:01:11,130
is, "What's the recommended
temperature to use?

1473
01:01:11,130 --> 01:01:14,010
I've generally found it to be
0.6 is what most people use.

1474
01:01:14,010 --> 01:01:17,850
I don't think that there's
something that's set in stone.

1475
01:01:17,850 --> 01:01:21,710
You can always change
it in some ways.

1476
01:01:21,710 --> 01:01:23,410
So the question is,
what's a solid line?

1477
01:01:23,410 --> 01:01:27,070
That's just SFT on human-written
solutions in this setting.

1478
01:01:27,070 --> 01:01:28,850
There's no advantage
in that line.

1479
01:01:28,850 --> 01:01:31,298
And the first-step
advantage one was actually

1480
01:01:31,298 --> 01:01:32,590
the one I showed to you before.

1481
01:01:32,590 --> 01:01:34,110
I removed it here because--

1482
01:01:34,110 --> 01:01:36,327
so this is maybe
the ultimate kind

1483
01:01:36,327 --> 01:01:37,910
of result in some
ways from human data

1484
01:01:37,910 --> 01:01:39,890
to self-generated data sorts.

1485
01:01:39,890 --> 01:01:43,460
But yeah, it's like 2x
with the first step.

1486
01:01:43,460 --> 01:01:45,500
Yeah.

1487
01:01:45,500 --> 01:01:46,120
OK.

1488
01:01:46,120 --> 01:01:47,580
So the final thing--

1489
01:01:47,580 --> 01:01:49,080
that final main
method, and then I'm

1490
01:01:49,080 --> 01:01:51,163
going to just talk about
what people are currently

1491
01:01:51,163 --> 01:01:53,680
doing very briefly, and I'll
end the lecture at that point.

1492
01:01:53,680 --> 01:01:55,305
But the final thing
we can also do here

1493
01:01:55,305 --> 01:01:56,888
is online
reinforcement learning.

1494
01:01:56,888 --> 01:01:58,180
There's two ways of doing this.

1495
01:01:58,180 --> 01:02:00,340
So the most basic
recipe for doing this

1496
01:02:00,340 --> 01:02:03,100
is actually to not use
any of this advantage,

1497
01:02:03,100 --> 01:02:05,460
but generalize this kind
of basic RFD algorithm

1498
01:02:05,460 --> 01:02:07,900
without advantages to
online [INAUDIBLE].

1499
01:02:07,900 --> 01:02:08,840
So how do we do that?

1500
01:02:08,840 --> 01:02:13,060
You can basically
just take your model,

1501
01:02:13,060 --> 01:02:14,940
similar to how we
sampled a bunch of traces

1502
01:02:14,940 --> 01:02:17,020
from my current model
or current policy,

1503
01:02:17,020 --> 01:02:18,500
and filtered the right ones.

1504
01:02:18,500 --> 01:02:21,520
Rather than filtering, you can
just train it with on-policy RL.

1505
01:02:21,520 --> 01:02:23,420
So you can just
generate rollouts

1506
01:02:23,420 --> 01:02:28,540
from your current policy, which
might be correct or wrong.

1507
01:02:28,540 --> 01:02:30,820
You can reward them
using the reward function

1508
01:02:30,820 --> 01:02:33,220
by just checking the
answer against the answer

1509
01:02:33,220 --> 01:02:34,472
that the model produces.

1510
01:02:34,472 --> 01:02:36,680
You can penalize it if it
does not produce an answer.

1511
01:02:36,680 --> 01:02:38,820
You can penalize it
if it violates format.

1512
01:02:38,820 --> 01:02:41,500
There's a bunch of
reward-shaping things

1513
01:02:41,500 --> 01:02:42,620
you can do.

1514
01:02:42,620 --> 01:02:44,580
And then you just
train your policy

1515
01:02:44,580 --> 01:02:47,740
to maximize this reward
using policy gradients.

1516
01:02:47,740 --> 01:02:52,343
So RFT, we were explicitly
filtering and training with SFT.

1517
01:02:52,343 --> 01:02:54,760
But rather than doing this
filtering or training with SFT,

1518
01:02:54,760 --> 01:02:56,860
you can simply run
on-policy reinforcement

1519
01:02:56,860 --> 01:02:59,180
learning because all
in this formulation

1520
01:02:59,180 --> 01:03:00,600
is just state action.

1521
01:03:00,600 --> 01:03:01,280
There's an MDP.

1522
01:03:01,280 --> 01:03:04,140
There's a reward and
just do r on top of it.

1523
01:03:04,140 --> 01:03:07,600
So you can simply write the
policy gradient equation,

1524
01:03:07,600 --> 01:03:09,500
so that basically just
looks like grad log

1525
01:03:09,500 --> 01:03:12,740
pi times the reward
that you see up here.

1526
01:03:12,740 --> 01:03:15,860
And you can just run
that on your problem.

1527
01:03:15,860 --> 01:03:17,700
There's also a lot
of work which now

1528
01:03:17,700 --> 01:03:21,220
tries to go beyond the basic
reinforce style policy gradient

1529
01:03:21,220 --> 01:03:24,220
and goes to methods
like what's called GRPO.

1530
01:03:24,220 --> 01:03:27,500
It's nothing but just an
extension of the basic PPO

1531
01:03:27,500 --> 01:03:29,400
algorithm in this setting.

1532
01:03:29,400 --> 01:03:30,857
So the PPO algorithm--

1533
01:03:30,857 --> 01:03:32,440
I guess I'm going
to say a few things.

1534
01:03:32,440 --> 01:03:34,840
PPO algorithm uses a
learned value function.

1535
01:03:34,840 --> 01:03:38,050
The GRP algorithm replaces
that learned value function

1536
01:03:38,050 --> 01:03:39,690
or learned Q-value
function with simply

1537
01:03:39,690 --> 01:03:42,130
this rollout-based
estimation of advantages.

1538
01:03:42,130 --> 01:03:44,570
So the same old basic
RL recipe applied

1539
01:03:44,570 --> 01:03:47,390
on an MDP that looks like the
thing that you see up there.

1540
01:03:47,390 --> 01:03:50,015
So a trajectory that looks like
the thing that you see up there

1541
01:03:50,015 --> 01:03:51,490
with a 01 reward.

1542
01:03:51,490 --> 01:03:52,150
OK.

1543
01:03:52,150 --> 01:03:53,713
And what you can
also do-- and this

1544
01:03:53,713 --> 01:03:55,130
is something that
finally can do--

1545
01:03:55,130 --> 01:03:58,730
is you can also do per-step
advantages in online RL.

1546
01:03:58,730 --> 01:04:01,130
That's the final method
that you can apply.

1547
01:04:01,130 --> 01:04:04,370
So in this setting you can take
a solution from your base model

1548
01:04:04,370 --> 01:04:06,895
or take a solution from
your current policy

1549
01:04:06,895 --> 01:04:07,770
that you're training.

1550
01:04:07,770 --> 01:04:09,530
Or over the course
of training, you

1551
01:04:09,530 --> 01:04:11,370
can do these rollouts
if you want to.

1552
01:04:11,370 --> 01:04:14,250
You can use these rollouts
to compute advantages.

1553
01:04:14,250 --> 01:04:15,910
And rather than
doing these rollouts,

1554
01:04:15,910 --> 01:04:18,570
you can also potentially do
something slightly different,

1555
01:04:18,570 --> 01:04:22,010
which is basically to
train a reward model-like

1556
01:04:22,010 --> 01:04:24,290
thing to fit to these
advantage predictions

1557
01:04:24,290 --> 01:04:25,970
or to fit to these
Q-value predictions

1558
01:04:25,970 --> 01:04:27,890
and then compute advantages.

1559
01:04:27,890 --> 01:04:30,370
So this is very similar
to some of the RHLF work

1560
01:04:30,370 --> 01:04:32,970
that you probably saw,
except that now this

1561
01:04:32,970 --> 01:04:36,750
is a reward model that is
rewarding every step of sorts,

1562
01:04:36,750 --> 01:04:39,950
or reward model that's producing
the Q-value for every step,

1563
01:04:39,950 --> 01:04:41,690
or the advantage
for every step, as

1564
01:04:41,690 --> 01:04:43,970
opposed to producing a
reward for the whole sequence

1565
01:04:43,970 --> 01:04:45,170
that you're seeing.

1566
01:04:45,170 --> 01:04:48,010
And once you have these
advantage predictions

1567
01:04:48,010 --> 01:04:52,730
that you can fit to a network,
you can query this particular--

1568
01:04:52,730 --> 01:04:55,570
so for any rollout that
your policy produces,

1569
01:04:55,570 --> 01:04:58,690
you can query the
advantage predictor

1570
01:04:58,690 --> 01:05:01,690
that you have trained to
reward every single step

1571
01:05:01,690 --> 01:05:03,810
in this particular
policy generation.

1572
01:05:03,810 --> 01:05:05,850
And once you do that,
what you can do now

1573
01:05:05,850 --> 01:05:07,950
is you can simply
take your network,

1574
01:05:07,950 --> 01:05:11,250
produce these advantage
labels, and optimize for reward

1575
01:05:11,250 --> 01:05:13,810
that you're getting at the
end, plus these advantages

1576
01:05:13,810 --> 01:05:16,478
that you see up here
in this setting.

1577
01:05:16,478 --> 01:05:17,270
Yeah, that's great.

1578
01:05:17,270 --> 01:05:19,437
So the question is, "How
generalizable is reasoning?

1579
01:05:19,437 --> 01:05:21,743
When you train on MATH,
does it improve on GSM8K?"

1580
01:05:21,743 --> 01:05:23,410
So it turns out if
you train on MATH, it

1581
01:05:23,410 --> 01:05:24,770
does not improve on GSM8K.

1582
01:05:24,770 --> 01:05:26,110
And many people have shown it.

1583
01:05:26,110 --> 01:05:29,010
But if you train on a bunch
of mixture of prompts,

1584
01:05:29,010 --> 01:05:30,785
which are of varied
levels of difficulty,

1585
01:05:30,785 --> 01:05:31,910
you can actually get there.

1586
01:05:31,910 --> 01:05:33,610
So this is precisely
the recipe, which

1587
01:05:33,610 --> 01:05:36,260
is being used in--
and/or not this one,

1588
01:05:36,260 --> 01:05:39,200
but the basic online recipe
which I talked to you about

1589
01:05:39,200 --> 01:05:41,440
earlier, used to
train all of these,

1590
01:05:41,440 --> 01:05:44,000
or many of these
thinking models which

1591
01:05:44,000 --> 01:05:46,822
have open recipe descriptions.

1592
01:05:46,822 --> 01:05:49,280
So this online recipe is kind
of used to train the DeepSeek

1593
01:05:49,280 --> 01:05:50,405
carbon model, for instance.

1594
01:05:50,405 --> 01:05:53,700
And that does generalize to a
fairly good amount of extent.

1595
01:05:53,700 --> 01:05:56,560
It doesn't also generalize
to every single problem

1596
01:05:56,560 --> 01:05:59,440
out there, but to a fairly
good extent it does.

1597
01:05:59,440 --> 01:06:02,020
So you can compute these
advantage predictions.

1598
01:06:02,020 --> 01:06:03,680
You can fit them
to a network, use

1599
01:06:03,680 --> 01:06:05,940
them to produce these rewards
for every single step.

1600
01:06:05,940 --> 01:06:09,015
And that's the more advanced
way of doing online RL

1601
01:06:09,015 --> 01:06:10,140
with this particular thing.

1602
01:06:10,140 --> 01:06:13,000
So we had a work that we called
process advantage verifiers

1603
01:06:13,000 --> 01:06:13,680
here.

1604
01:06:13,680 --> 01:06:16,480
A process because it's
rewarding every step;

1605
01:06:16,480 --> 01:06:18,880
advantage because it's
fitting an advantage function;

1606
01:06:18,880 --> 01:06:23,500
and a verifier because, somehow
in the domain of reasoning,

1607
01:06:23,500 --> 01:06:25,980
reward models are referred
to often as verifiers.

1608
01:06:25,980 --> 01:06:28,400
So if you see that
word come up, it's

1609
01:06:28,400 --> 01:06:30,540
just synonymous to a
reward model of sorts.

1610
01:06:30,540 --> 01:06:32,120
Or in this particular
case, even you

1611
01:06:32,120 --> 01:06:34,760
can think of it as a Q function
or a value function of sorts.

1612
01:06:34,760 --> 01:06:36,460
But that's the
name of the method.

1613
01:06:36,460 --> 01:06:38,628
And we find that in general--

1614
01:06:38,628 --> 01:06:40,420
so I'm going to skip
some of these details.

1615
01:06:40,420 --> 01:06:44,120
But in general, if you
do dense reward RL,

1616
01:06:44,120 --> 01:06:47,480
like process reward or
advantage-based reward RL,

1617
01:06:47,480 --> 01:06:50,640
you find that you can get
to a much better accuracy,

1618
01:06:50,640 --> 01:06:54,725
compared to what's called ORM
model or just 01 one reward RL,

1619
01:06:54,725 --> 01:06:56,600
like the sparse 01 reward
provided at the end

1620
01:06:56,600 --> 01:06:57,600
of the trajectory.

1621
01:06:57,600 --> 01:07:00,440
So this is the basic recipe
that a lot of the current work

1622
01:07:00,440 --> 01:07:01,260
is using.

1623
01:07:01,260 --> 01:07:03,860
This is the advantage-based
verifiers recipe.

1624
01:07:03,860 --> 01:07:05,760
And you can see that
you can get a bigger

1625
01:07:05,760 --> 01:07:07,520
boost in sample
efficiency, as well

1626
01:07:07,520 --> 01:07:09,960
as in terms of just
absolute performance

1627
01:07:09,960 --> 01:07:11,160
that you see up here.

1628
01:07:11,160 --> 01:07:15,920
Also, these numbers look small,
but with 2B and 9B models that

1629
01:07:15,920 --> 01:07:18,840
we were basing this off of,
apparently this is the best

1630
01:07:18,840 --> 01:07:21,880
result that you can get on
certain data sets that we tried

1631
01:07:21,880 --> 01:07:22,720
this on.

1632
01:07:22,720 --> 01:07:25,840
So it's about 5 or 6x sample
efficiency boost and about 6%

1633
01:07:25,840 --> 01:07:29,280
to 7% absolute performance
improvement in this particular

1634
01:07:29,280 --> 01:07:32,020
setting with the dense
advantages in some way.

1635
01:07:32,020 --> 01:07:35,350

1636
01:07:35,350 --> 01:07:37,210
OK, I'm going to skip
some of these things.

1637
01:07:37,210 --> 01:07:42,030
But at a high level, we are also
seeing now these newer thinking

1638
01:07:42,030 --> 01:07:44,830
models that have come up where
basically these models can just

1639
01:07:44,830 --> 01:07:47,250
think for longer and
produce a better answer.

1640
01:07:47,250 --> 01:07:49,270
This is the DeepSeek-R1
family of models,

1641
01:07:49,270 --> 01:07:54,790
OpenAI family of models or O
series of models from OpenAI.

1642
01:07:54,790 --> 01:07:57,430
In my opinion, really,
these are trained

1643
01:07:57,430 --> 01:07:59,838
using very similar
recipes that I just

1644
01:07:59,838 --> 01:08:01,630
talked to you about
for this whole lecture,

1645
01:08:01,630 --> 01:08:03,505
except that there are
some small changes that

1646
01:08:03,505 --> 01:08:08,630
happen in this process
from pre these models

1647
01:08:08,630 --> 01:08:10,390
versus these models.

1648
01:08:10,390 --> 01:08:12,970
So the RL training objective
has remained the same.

1649
01:08:12,970 --> 01:08:14,310
So there's some minor details.

1650
01:08:14,310 --> 01:08:16,069
Like, there's this
new method called

1651
01:08:16,069 --> 01:08:18,130
TRPO which I just describing.

1652
01:08:18,130 --> 01:08:20,270
It's basically PPO
with some tweaks.

1653
01:08:20,270 --> 01:08:23,630
There's also some other methods
like advantage-induced policy

1654
01:08:23,630 --> 01:08:24,130
alignment.

1655
01:08:24,130 --> 01:08:26,050
You don't need to know
what those methods are.

1656
01:08:26,050 --> 01:08:27,710
Just think of them
roughly as policy

1657
01:08:27,710 --> 01:08:30,830
gradient methods done with
slight tweaks overall.

1658
01:08:30,830 --> 01:08:34,350
But what has changed is that
the action space in a way

1659
01:08:34,350 --> 01:08:36,550
has changed for these
models, in my opinion.

1660
01:08:36,550 --> 01:08:38,750
Now, this is a very
crude statement to make.

1661
01:08:38,750 --> 01:08:41,333
And to be really
rigorous about it,

1662
01:08:41,333 --> 01:08:42,750
I think the statement
sounds wrong

1663
01:08:42,750 --> 01:08:45,229
because these models do actually
produce token by token one

1664
01:08:45,229 --> 01:08:46,149
at a time.

1665
01:08:46,149 --> 01:08:48,850
But these models, the base
models on which we are doing RL,

1666
01:08:48,850 --> 01:08:53,590
have become better at being able
to implement interesting meta

1667
01:08:53,590 --> 01:08:54,910
procedures.

1668
01:08:54,910 --> 01:08:56,803
These models can actually
produce a solution,

1669
01:08:56,803 --> 01:08:59,470
maybe go out there and then try
to verify the solution they have

1670
01:08:59,470 --> 01:09:01,637
produced, maybe then backtrack
to a particular point

1671
01:09:01,637 --> 01:09:03,130
and do something
else from there.

1672
01:09:03,130 --> 01:09:05,338
And you can think of this
as just yet another version

1673
01:09:05,338 --> 01:09:06,350
of defining the step.

1674
01:09:06,350 --> 01:09:08,830
These models can produce
more interesting steps

1675
01:09:08,830 --> 01:09:12,029
than just going out there and
calculating yet another quantity

1676
01:09:12,029 --> 01:09:14,590
like the models previously
could have done.

1677
01:09:14,590 --> 01:09:17,790
So it's just a better notion
of a step or a better action

1678
01:09:17,790 --> 01:09:19,950
that these models
can sample when

1679
01:09:19,950 --> 01:09:22,830
you run all of these
algorithms on top of them.

1680
01:09:22,830 --> 01:09:26,029
And once you do that, and you
give these models a longer

1681
01:09:26,029 --> 01:09:28,990
budget to act-- so if you tell
these models that, OK, there's

1682
01:09:28,990 --> 01:09:31,819
no limit on how many
tokens you can think,

1683
01:09:31,819 --> 01:09:33,978
you can sample during
the course of RL,

1684
01:09:33,978 --> 01:09:36,520
you can maybe sample solutions
which are really, really long,

1685
01:09:36,520 --> 01:09:38,120
but just get me
the right answer.

1686
01:09:38,120 --> 01:09:39,819
If I give these
models that playground

1687
01:09:39,819 --> 01:09:42,220
to sample more and
more tokens, turns out

1688
01:09:42,220 --> 01:09:46,140
that RL training algorithms
with these two bullet points

1689
01:09:46,140 --> 01:09:48,165
actually give you much
better performance.

1690
01:09:48,165 --> 01:09:50,540
And that's what we see with
these current thinking models

1691
01:09:50,540 --> 01:09:51,939
right now.

1692
01:09:51,939 --> 01:09:54,020
It turns out that
these models are--

1693
01:09:54,020 --> 01:09:57,060
if you do a counterfactual
experiment where you take models

1694
01:09:57,060 --> 01:10:00,380
which are not capable of
implementing like basic

1695
01:10:00,380 --> 01:10:04,080
answer-checking or verification
or generating a better answer,

1696
01:10:04,080 --> 01:10:08,740
it turns out that you cannot
do good RL with them right now.

1697
01:10:08,740 --> 01:10:10,660
So really, nothing
has changed in terms

1698
01:10:10,660 --> 01:10:13,640
of the high-level techniques
that I talked about,

1699
01:10:13,640 --> 01:10:17,540
except that the base models have
become more interesting in a way

1700
01:10:17,540 --> 01:10:20,813
that they can sample interesting
kinds of steps to train on.

1701
01:10:20,813 --> 01:10:22,980
And that has led to some
of the most amazing results

1702
01:10:22,980 --> 01:10:25,330
